The removal of acetylene from gaseous feedstock prior to further processing is critical as acetylene is an explosive gas that possesses the widest flammability range among reported gases.[1] Furthermore, acetylene which is highly reactive may cause an undesirable chemical reaction in various industrial processes. For example, a trace amount of acetylene has been known to poison catalysts by forming metal-acetylides during the polymerization of ethylene.[2] Meanwhile, acetylene recovered in high purity can be served as a valuable resource such as a fuel and a building block for polymer synthesis. Among the various recovery and purification methods, adsorption using porous materials has been demonstrated to be feasible to separate this
