explosive gas from other gases in an efficient manner.[3] In this context, the development of acetylene-selective adsorbent materials is highly desirable for various niche applications.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) or porous coordination polymers (PCPs) have been emerged as a promising material for the adsorptive separation of various gases.[4] It is because both chemical and structural properties which affect the separation efficiency in adsorptive gas separation are tunable by judiciously selecting the metal source and the organic linkers. In this regard, several microporous MOFs that can selectively take up acetylene from other gases having a similar physical property with acetylene, such as carbon dioxide[5] or ethylene,[6] have been reported recently. However, MOFs showing a high selectivity toward acetylene over both carbon dioxide and ethylene are relatively rare.[6b, 7] The best performing example is a flexible MOF named UTSA-300a which is reported by B. Chen and co-workers in 2017.[7b] It was postulated that the strong hydrogen bonding interaction between UTSA-300a and acetylene triggers a structural change to an open form, while remaining closed for other gases such as carbon dioxide or ethylene to afford such a high ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST) selectivity. However, the IAST selectivity from the single component sorption isotherms may not simply applied to the selective separation from the gas mixture in the flexible MOF due to the gate opening.
To increase the interaction between acetylene and MOFs, we turned our attention to an imidazolium functionality. Imidazolium is widely used as a precursor of N-heterocyclic carbene (NHC) as well as the cationic part of ionic liquids.[8] Therefore, a number of MOFs bearing the imidazolium group have been reported for the past decade for the potential applications in catalysis and material science.[9] In this work, we attempted to mimic the properties of ionic liquids by constructing imidazolium-based MOFs, as ionic liquids may effectively discriminate gas molecules based on the differences in solubility.[10] It is noteworthy that certain ionic liquids exhibited a high acetylene solubility owing to hydrogen bonding interaction between imidazolium salt and acetylene molecule.[11]
Herein, we report a new cationic microporous MOF $[\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{P z l m})(\mathsf{N O}_{3})]$ (JCM-1, H2PzIm = 1,3-bis(3,5-dimethyl-1Hpyrazol-4-yl)-1H-imidazol-3-ium) that possesses aligned imidazolium groups along the pore channels and the pyrazolate metal binding site allowing a strong metal to ligand bond. The structure of JCM-1 was confirmed by single-crystal X-ray diffraction analysis. Gas adsorption study revealed that JCM-1 has a highly acetylene-selective sorption property from either carbon dioxide or ethylene. The effective separation of these gases as well as the recyclability were also demonstrated by column breakthrough experiments employing binary mixtures of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ . Most importantly, to the best of our knowledge, JCM-1 is the first imidazolium-based MOF possessing an excellent water-stability, a key property required for practical applications in realistic gas separation processes.
JCM-1 crystallizes in a three-dimensional framework with tetragonal I41/amd space group. In the crystal structure, each cobalt ion has a tetrahedral geometry connected by four different pyrazolate units, and the cobalt ions construct 4-fold helical chain secondary building units (SBUs) along the a axis (Figure 1a and 1b). The framework has one-dimensional channels running through the c direction with a diameter of $12.5\times3.9\mathbb{A}$ (Figure 1c and 1d). Since the charge of the cobalt metal node $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{p}z_{0.5})_{4}$ (pz $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ pyrazolate) is neutral, the cationic imidazolium ligand results in the formation of a net cationic 3D framework with nitrate as a counter anion. The nitrate anion was assigned in the single-crystal structure (Figure S2), and the presence of nitrate was further confirmed by the IR spectra, energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS), and elemental analysis of JCM-1 (Figure S13 and S14). The permanent porosity of JCM-1 was confirmed by ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption data at 77 K (Figure S5). The resulting sorption isotherm shows a type I profile with an uptake capacity of $150~\mathrm{mL}$ ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ under 0.2 bar. The type I isotherm indicates the microporosity of JCM-1, as observed in the single crystal structure. The BET surface area of JCM-1 was calculated to be $550~\mathsf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ from the adsorption isotherm.
The $C_{2}H_{2}$ , $C_{2}H_{4}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ gas sorption isotherms of JCM-1 were measured at $273\mathsf{K}$ and $298\mathsf{K}.$ As shown in Figure 2a and 2c, ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ uptake in JCM-1 is higher than those of $C_{2}H_{4}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ at both temperatures. At 298 K, the adsorption amount of three gases was slightly decreased, but similar acetylene selectivity was maintained. Notably, the sorption data show synchronous selectivity toward $C_{2}H_{2}$ over $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and $C_{2}H_{4}$ . Under ambient condition (298 K, 1 bar), C2H2 uptake capacity $(75\mathsf{m L9}^{-1})$ of JCM1 is almost twice of sorption capacities of $\mathsf{S O}_{2}\ (38\ \mathsf{m L}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}.$ and $C_{2}H_{4}$ $(35\mathsf{m L}\mathsf{g}^{-1}.$ ). The unique sorption property of JCM-1 can be explained by the proper size fit of the channel window and ionic ligand moiety decorated on the pore surfaces. The appropriate channel diameter $(3.9{\mathring{\mathsf{A}}})$ can attribute the selective sorption of acetylene $(3.3\mathring\mathsf{A})$ over ethylene $(4.2\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ . Not only the size effect, as studied with the imidazolium-based ionic liquids, but the high affinity of imidazolium functional group toward acetylene gives rise to such selective sorption property.
The adsorption selectivity can be predicted from single component adsorption isotherm of each species by adapting ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST).[12] The selectivities of binary gas mixtures (50:50 v/v and $1{:}99\ \mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ of $C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}$ and $C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}$ calculated by IAST are depicted in Figure 2d and Figure S7, respectively. The predicted $C_{2}H_{2}$ selectivities over $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and $C_{2}H_{4}$ $(50;50~\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ are 13.7 and 13.2 respectively at $298\mathsf{K}$ and $100\mathsf{k P a}.$ JCM-1’s acetylene uptake capacity and high selectivity toward carbon dioxide and ethylene are comparable with other reported materials (Table S5). The zero-coverage isosteric heats $(Q_{\mathrm{st}})$ of adsorption for $C_{2}H_{2}$ , $C_{2}H_{4}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ were calculated to be 36.9, 34.2, and 33.4 kJ mol-1 respectively utilizing the ClausiusClapeyron equation based on the fittings using dual-site Langmuir model (Figure 2b). Importantly, the difference in $Q_{\mathrm{st}}$ values for $C_{2}H_{2}$ and $C_{2}H_{4}$ adsorption on JCM-1 were not significant, indicating that the $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{4}$ selectivity was originated from the dissimilar interaction with the functional pore surfaces as well as size-exclusive effect from narrow channel window $(3.9\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ of JCM1. It is worth noting that $\mathsf{Q}_{\mathsf{s t}}$ of acetylene was maintained up to the loading of c.a. $2.5\ \mathsf{m m o l}\ \mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , which is reasonaby close to the amount of nitrate in JCM-1 (calculated to be $2.66\ m m{0}|\ g^{-1})$ , indicating a potential role of nitrate for the strong binding of acetylene. In the case of ${\sf C}_{2}{\sf H}_{2}/{\sf C}{\sf O}_{2}$ selectivity, considering that $C_{2}H_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ have very similar kinetic diameters $(3.3{\mathring\mathsf{A}})$ , we assume that the differential interaction energies within the pore surface can be a major factor of the selectivity. As expected, $Q_{\mathsf{s t}}$ value of $C_{2}H_{2}$ in JCM-1 was quite higher than that of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ at the low coverage region which is agreeable with a higher affinity of JCM-1 toward $C_{2}H_{2}$ than $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ .
To understand the origin of the affinity toward $C_{2}H_{2}$ over $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ within JCM-1, the interaction energies between imidazolium nitrate of JCM-1 and gas molecules were calculated using density functional theory (DFT) calculations. The coordinates of an appropriate pore structure with a nitrate anion were generated from the single crystal X-ray data and subsequently the initial coordinates of acetylene or carbon dioxide were loaded into the structure for the further optimization (Figure S8). In the optimized structures, the imidazolium and nitrate group shows stronger molecular interaction with ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ than with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , as the interaction enthalpies of $C_{2}H_{2}$ (hydrogen bonding interaction) and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ (dipolar Interaction) were computed to be $47.2\mathrm{kJ}\mathsf{m o l}^{-1}$ and 28.5 kJ mol-1, respectively.
In addition, the interaction between the nitrate anion and acetylene molecule inside JCM-1 was experimentally determined by IR spectroscopy and sorption experiment. When acetylene gas was loaded into the JCM-1, the nitrate peak $(1328\ \mathsf{c m}^{-1})$ ) was broadened and slightly blue-shifted by $4~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ , while other peaks from the framework remained intact (Figure S16). This shift indicates hydrogen bonding formation between nitrate and acetylene molecule.[13] To evaluate the effect of the nitrate anion, we also carried out gas sorption experiments with JCM-1 of which nitrate anion was replaced with chloride anion. The successful anion exchange of JCM-1 was confirmed by powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), IR spectroscopy, and EDS analysis (Figure S17-19). Sorption data of JCM-1(Cl-) with $C_{2}H_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ show that the heat of adsorption for $C_{2}H_{2}$ in JCM-1(Cl-) is lower than that of JCM- $1(\mathsf{N O}_{3}\mathsf{^{-}})$ by $\sim10\ensuremath{\upkappa}\cup\ensuremath{\mathsf{m o l}^{-1}}$ (Figure S20b). The predicted ${\sf C}_{2}{\sf H}_{2}/{\sf C}{\sf O}_{2}$ (50:50 v/v) selectivity was also significantly decreased from 13.7 to 7.4 at $298~\mathsf{K}$ and $100~\mathsf{k P a}$ after anion exchange (Figure S20c). These theoretical and experimental observations imply that the strong hydrogen bond between imidazolium nitrate and $C_{2}H_{2}$ could be the origin of the unique selectivity of the framework.
Although a substantial number of MOFs with an imidazolium moiety were reported thus far, the stability study of MOFs toward water or moisture has not yet been reported. In view of the importance of the water stability of MOFs for the practical applications,[13] the water stability of JCM-1 was investigated. Long-lasting water stability of JCM-1 was tested by soaking activated crystals in water and monitoring the crystallinity with Xray diffraction (PXRD) for a lengthy period of time. As shown in Figure 3, PXRD patterns of JCM-1 soaked in water were not changed even after 2 months. Furthermore, the water vapor sorption isotherm experiment confirms that JCM-1 can adsorb water molecule inside its pore while the structure of JCM-1 was well retained after vapor sorption (Figure S15). This can be attributed to the strong metal to pyrazolate bond in the JCM-1 which has been proven to enhance the moisture stability.[13b, 13c] Besides, the presence of hydrophobic methyl groups in the ligands and continuous cobalt-pyrazolate chains might hinder the access of water molecules to the metal sites (Figure 1b), resulting in a better resistance of the framework towards water.[13c, 14]
The $C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}$ and $C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}$ separation performances of JCM-1 were also examined through experimental breakthrough studies using a custom-built system reported previously.[15] The equimolar gas mixtures were passed over a tubular column packed with activated JCM-1 solid with a flow rate of 7 mL min-1 at $298\textsf{K}$ (Figure S9). The breakthrough data depicted in Figure 4a clearly demonstrate that JCM-1 can effectively separate ${\sf C}_{2}{\sf H}_{2}/{\sf C}{\sf O}_{2}$ mixture even at a dynamic condition. In this dynamic breakthrough testing with equimolar mixture of $C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}$ , only $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , which has lower affinity with JCM-1 appears at the outlet of column at the initial stage. The flow of pure $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ continues until the mass transfer zone of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ that propagates along the column reaches the end of the column. The adsorption capacity of $C_{2}H_{2}$ in JCM-1 under dynamic condition was calculated to be 2.2 mmol ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ , which is fairly close to the equilibrium adsorption of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ under the same condition (2.7 mmol ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ at 0.5 bar). The ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ selectivity of JCM-1 under this condition was calculated to be 4.4. The selectivity further increased to 11.4 for the ${\sf C}_{2}{\sf H}_{2}/{\sf C}{\sf O}_{2}$ mixture gas with the ratio of 1:99 (Figure S12). The breakthrough experiment was also conducted with an equimolar mixture of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ at the same condition (Figure S10). In this case, the $C_{2}H_{2}$ capacity and $C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}$ selectivity were measured to be 1.7 mmol ${\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ and 3.1, respectively, indicating a moderate separation ability under a dynamic separation condition.
Besides, the capability of JCM-1 under repetitive adsorptiondesorption cycles, which is critical for practical application, was evaluated using cycling breakthrough experiments with an equimolar mixture of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ at $298\mathsf{K}$ and 1 bar. JCM-1 showed a decent regenerability even at a mild desorption condition (373 K for 2 hours), such that a marginal decrease in $C_{2}H_{2}$ capacity (less than $10\%$ ) was observed over 12 cycles (Figure 4b and Figure S11). A good recyclability was also confirmed by multiple repetitions of isotherm measurements as shown in Figure S6. All these results strongly suggest that JCM-1 possess an excellent structural stability as well as near-intact ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ adsorption capability.
In conclusion, we have synthesized a new imidazolium-based microporous MOF (JCM-1) that exhibits a high ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ selectivity over $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and $C_{2}H_{4}$ in adsorptive gas separations. The unique acetylene selectivity observed in this MOF is originated from the proper channel diameter and the strong interaction between ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ and ionic liquid moiety aligned through the pore channels. Furthermore, the long-lasting water stability of JCM-1 promises potential applications of the material in realistic conditions. This work demonstrates that imidazolium-based MOFs are applicable for the separation of hydrocarbons and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , and further investigations with the material for other challenging gas separation is in progress.
